  rankdir="TB";
  "N1" [label="(7,0,0) Currently a direct real-space proof of PSMTs at the atomic or molecular scale remains absent."];
  "N13" [label="(21,0,0) Light irradiation can trigger a dramatic face changing of the surface, transforming it from the semiconducting Cu2Se layer into a close-packed Cu layer."];
  "N14" [label="(22,0,0) Upon thermal activation, the transition can be reversed to the original Cu2Se surface via segregation."];
  "N15" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given that light transforms Cu2Se to Cu and thermal activation reverses it, therefore a reversible photoinduced transition between two distinct materials (Cu2Se and Cu) has been experimentally achieved."];
  "N16" [label="(23,0,0) The calculated Cu2Se-to-Cu transition barrier is significantly lower in the excited state than in the ground state."];
  "N17" [label="(31,0,0) The photoinduced electronically excited state plays a crucial role in the semiconductor-to-metal transition."];
  "N18" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given the crucial role of the excited state and the lowered transition barrier, therefore the photoinduced transition is fundamentally driven by the surface entering an electronically excited state."];
  "N19" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given the experimental achievement of the Cu2Se-to-Cu transition and its verified excited-state mechanism, therefore we have successfully demonstrated a complete, reversible PSMT between two distinct materials."];
  "N2" [label="(8,0,0) All reported PSMTs occurred between two structural phases of the same material, with no precedent for PSMTs between two different materials."];
  "N3" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given that atomic-scale proof is absent and transitions only occur within the same material, therefore there is a critical research gap in achieving atomic-scale PSMTs between distinct materials."];
  "N4" [label="(10,0,0) If surface reconstruction occurs, then it refers to structural changes in the surface lateral atomic arrangement accompanied by varied vertical interlayer spacing."];
  "N5" [label="(11,0,0) Currently this transformation significantly affects the chemical and physical nature of materials."];
  "N6" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given that surface reconstruction changes atomic arrangements and affects chemical/physical nature, therefore surface reconstruction is a mechanism capable of transforming materials at the atomic scale."];
  "N7" [label="(16,0,0) Copper selenide (Cu2Se) is an intrinsic semiconductor."];
  "N8" [label="(17,0,0) Its thin flake is known as a typical two-dimensional (2D) transition metal chalcogenide."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper investigates and demonstrates atomic-scale evidence for a reversible photoinduced phase change on a Cu(111) surface covered by single-layer Cu2Se: photoexcitation drives a semiconductor-to-metal transition by lowering the activation barrier for Se atoms to sink into the subsurface and Cu atoms to form a close-packed metallic Cu layer, thereby converting semiconducting Cu2Se into metallic Cu and addressing the gap in distinct-material PSMTs.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) The retained and repaired terminal conclusions jointly motivate the paper-level research proposal."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Cu2Se combines intrinsic semiconducting character with a two-dimensional transition-metal-chalcogenide form."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Because Cu2Se is a semiconducting two-dimensional material and surface reconstruction can transform surface atomic arrangements and material properties, Cu2Se surface reconstruction is a plausible route to investigate for atomic-scale material transformation."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) The gap in atomic-scale photoinduced phase transitions between distinct materials motivates investigating Cu2Se surface reconstruction as a possible strategy for addressing that gap."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) The demonstrated reversible Cu2Se-to-Cu transition, together with the identified absence of prior atomic-scale proof for distinct-material PSMTs, supports the claim that this work addresses the gap by providing evidence for a reversible photoinduced transition between distinct materials."];
